winebus: on Bluetooth, ride the client's packet and take the pad's own path.

Two options become defaults, on Bluetooth only. Neither is new code; both have
been off since the day they were written, waiting for the thing that would
decide them.

THE RIDE, mgvf-0020. It stamps the motors into a 0x31 the game is sending
anyway, so nothing of ours is added to a link that drains about sixty-five
reports a second. Measured on Beast of Reincarnation: 92-95% of every motor
change carried inside the client's own packet, no packet of ours on the link,
and the frame cost that started this whole series gone. A cable keeps it off,
which is not a preference: the ride stamps a 0x31 and a wired pad is written
0x02s, so there is nothing there to ride.

THE STOP, WHICH IS THE PART THAT HAD TO BE CHECKED BEFORE THIS COULD SHIP. A
ride that cannot deliver a zero leaves the pad buzzing in a menu, and that is
the failure a person would report as "the rumble never stops" rather than as a
frame rate. Across the three runs of 2026-09-11 -- hid-235325, hid-235541 and
hid-235817, 700 motor packets between them:

  episodes of non-zero rumble                  105
  of those, ended in a zero                    105
  delay from the last non-zero packet          median 17 ms, worst 100 ms
  traces ending with the pad silenced          3 of 3

mgvf-0018 exempts zero from the deadband in both directions, which is why a
stop is never the thing that gets held back.

WHAT IS STILL UNTESTED IN THE FIELD, and it is named rather than left implied:
the fallback for a client that stops writing in the middle of a rumble. That
title writes its own 0x31 about forty-five times a second without a pause,
menus included, so the ride always had a carrier and the thread's 100 ms
silence rule never once had to fire. The path exists and the host tests cover
its logic. No trace has ever exercised it.

THE HAPTIC PATH, mgvf-0021. It sent legacy because a six-pulse ladder by hand
had preferred legacy, and that ladder was run when this driver could deliver
about sixteen updates a second. At the sixty a second the ride now delivers,
the same hand preferred the haptic path: finer, and the frames stayed perfect.
It is also the path Sony's library takes on every packet of every session
captured here.

AND THE ONE THING THAT COULD HAVE MADE THAT COMPARISON UNFAIR IS NOW RULED OUT.
mgvf-0023 asked whether an unclaimed motor power field had been quietly
attenuating one path or both. Measured: claiming it at zero felt identical to
leaving it alone, and claiming it at 0x77 dropped the rumble to almost nothing.
The pad honours the field, it only reduces, and it was already at none. Both
paths were at full power the whole time, so the preference is a preference
about the paths and not about a hidden setting.

A CABLE KEEPS THE LEGACY PATH. Nothing measured says which is better there, and
a wired pad has none of the link pressure that made the comparison worth
re-running. Changing it would be a preference dressed as a finding.

ONE WARN BECOMES A TRACE. Asking for the haptic path while VibrationMode was 1
used to be asking for two opposite things, because mgvf-0009's rewrite runs
after the stamp and turns the packet back into the legacy motors. With the path
now a default, that same pair is the ordinary way to get the harder feel and
nobody has made a mistake, so the line says what happens instead of complaining
about it.

Both options still override the default in either direction, and
XInputRumbleRide 0 puts a Bluetooth pad back on the old behaviour without a
rebuild.

Signed-off-by: MacGameVideoFix
---
--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/main.c
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/main.c
@@ -2262,21 +2262,64 @@
         TRACE("device %p: a motor must move %u of 255 before the link is used\n",
               device, ext->haptics_deadband);
 
-    /* MGVF mgvf-0020: ride the client's packet. OFF until it is measured; the
-     * header of dualsense_usb.h says what it is for and why the packet has to
-     * be free rather than merely rare. */
-    /* MGVF mgvf-0021: which way the motors are asked to move. The default is
-     * the legacy path this driver has always used; 1 selects the pad's own
-     * haptic path, the one Sony's library uses on every packet. */
-    ext->haptics_path = get_device_option(ext->desc.vid, ext->desc.pid, L"XInputRumbleHapticPath", 0)
+    /* MGVF mgvf-0020: ride the client's packet. ON over Bluetooth and off on a
+     * cable, which is not a preference but the shape of the thing: the ride
+     * stamps a 0x31, and a wired pad is written 0x02s, so there is nothing to
+     * ride there.
+     *
+     * MGVF mgvf-0024 TURNED IT ON. It was off until it was measured, and it is
+     * measured: on Beast of Reincarnation over Bluetooth it carried 92-95% of
+     * every motor change inside a packet the game was sending anyway, added no
+     * packet of its own to a link that only drains about sixty-five a second,
+     * and the frame cost that started this whole series went away.
+     *
+     * AND THE STOP IS THE PART THAT HAD TO BE CHECKED, because a ride that
+     * cannot deliver a zero leaves the pad buzzing in a menu. Across the three
+     * runs of 2026-09-11, 105 episodes of non-zero rumble, every one of them
+     * ended in a zero -- median 17 ms after the last non-zero packet, worst
+     * 100 ms -- and all three traces end with the pad silenced. mgvf-0018
+     * exempts zero from the deadband in both directions, which is why.
+     *
+     * WHAT IS STILL UNTESTED IN THE FIELD, said rather than left implied: the
+     * fallback for a client that stops writing mid-rumble. That title writes
+     * its own 0x31 about forty-five times a second without pause, menus
+     * included, so the ride always had a carrier and the thread's 100 ms
+     * silence rule never once had to fire. The path exists and the host tests
+     * cover its logic; no trace has ever exercised it. */
+    /* MGVF mgvf-0021: which way the motors are asked to move.
+     *
+     * MGVF mgvf-0024 MADE THE HAPTIC PATH THE BLUETOOTH DEFAULT. mgvf-0021 sent
+     * legacy because a six-pulse ladder by hand had preferred it, and that
+     * ladder was run when this driver could deliver about sixteen updates a
+     * second. At the sixty a second mgvf-0020 delivers, the same hand preferred
+     * the haptic path: finer, and the frames stayed perfect. It is also the
+     * path Sony's own library takes on every packet of every session captured
+     * here, and mgvf-0023 has since ruled out the one thing that could have
+     * made that comparison unfair -- there was no hidden attenuator, the pad
+     * was at full power in both.
+     *
+     * A CABLE KEEPS THE LEGACY PATH. Nothing measured says which is better
+     * there, and a wired pad has none of the link pressure that made the
+     * comparison worth re-running; changing it would be a preference dressed
+     * as a finding. The option overrides either way. */
+    ext->haptics_path = get_device_option(ext->desc.vid, ext->desc.pid, L"XInputRumbleHapticPath",
+                                          dualsense_speaks_bluetooth(ext))
                         ? DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_PATH_HAPTIC : DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_PATH_LEGACY;
     if (ext->haptics_path == DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_PATH_HAPTIC)
     {
         TRACE("device %p: the motors are asked through the pad's haptic path, not the legacy motors\n", device);
+        /* MGVF mgvf-0024: this was a WARN while the haptic path was something
+         * a person had to ask for, because asking for it alongside a
+         * VibrationMode of 1 was asking for two opposite things. Now that the
+         * path is the Bluetooth default, the same pair is the ordinary way to
+         * get the harder feel: the stamp takes the haptic path and mgvf-0009's
+         * rewrite, which runs after it, turns the packet back into the legacy
+         * motors. Nobody made a mistake, so it says what happens and does not
+         * complain about it. */
         if (ext->dualsense_vibration.mode == DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_MODE_LEGACY)
-            WARN("device %p: VibrationMode asks for the legacy motors and XInputRumbleHapticPath asks for the"
-                 " haptic one; the rewrite runs after the stamp, so the legacy path wins and this option does"
-                 " nothing. Set the vibration to the game's own choice to compare the two paths.\n", device);
+            TRACE("device %p: VibrationMode 1 rewrites the packet after the stamp, so what reaches the pad is"
+                  " the legacy motors -- which is what that mode is for. Leave the vibration at the game's own"
+                  " choice for the haptic path.\n", device);
     }
 
     /* MGVF mgvf-0023: the motor power field. The option carries the BYTE and
@@ -2295,7 +2338,8 @@
         TRACE("device %p: the motor power field is left unclaimed -- the pad keeps whatever it was last"
               " told, by us or by anything else\n", device);
 
-    ext->haptics_ride = get_device_option(ext->desc.vid, ext->desc.pid, L"XInputRumbleRide", 0);
+    ext->haptics_ride = get_device_option(ext->desc.vid, ext->desc.pid, L"XInputRumbleRide",
+                                          dualsense_speaks_bluetooth(ext));
     ext->haptics_sent = -1;
     ext->haptics_client_seen = 0;
     ext->haptics_thread_writing = 0;
